	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037007157?origin=resultslist},
	affiliations = {Southeast University, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Dhaka, 1208, Bangladesh},
	abstract = {Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA) is not only a complex topic for programmers but also a major thing to learn in computer science. Students are facing challenges in their day to day life learning concepts of Data Structures and how Algorithms work. This study shows that how game development can be integrated in education and helps the students to understand complex topics such as Data Structures and Algorithms (DSA). To do that, we created a 3D game named Dijkstra Hallway Escape and the game is made with Unreal Engine 5, where players use the Algorithm of Dijkstra to escape a survival-based scenario. By playing this interactive game, the player can see the immediate effect of their choice if his calculation were right or wrong. A group of computer science students took part in a small pilot study consisting of pre-test and post-test. The findings revealed that the participants were better at understanding the Dijkstra algorithm by playing the game, as they got a better average score and greater confidence in implementing the algorithm. Players also made comments about how visualization and consequence-based gameplay helped them to learn how algorithms function in our real-life scenarios. The results may be an indication that game-based learning is potentially a strong method of teaching abstract computer science subjects, even though it is a very small group that was studied this early. © 2026 IEEE.},
